# Controlled Encapsulation of Functional Organic Molecules within Metal-Organic Frameworks: In Situ Crystalline Structure Transformation

## abstract
Functional organic molecules/metal-organic frameworks composites can be obtained by in situ crystalline structure transformation from ZIF-L to ZIF-8-L under double solvent conditions. Interestingly, the as-prepared molecules/ZIF-8-L composites with the leaf-like morphology exhibit good fluorescence properties and size selectivity in fluorescent quenchers due to the molecular sieving effect of the well-defined microporous ZIF-8-L.

Ordered nanoporous systems with a large surface area and adjustable pore environment are desirable in many applications, which can offer a broad platform for different kinds of functional species as well as an optimal condition for some special reactions.[1] Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs),[2] a new type of porous crystalline materials, are constructed by coordination of metal ions or clusters with organic ligands in appropriate solvents and have recently received considerable attention. Due to their large internal surface areas, uniform but tunable channel, and tailorable chemistry, MOFs can be applied in many fields, such as gas storage,[3] separation,[4] catalysis,[5] drug delivery,[6] and sensing.[7] More importantly, various functional species, including metal nanoparticles or oxides, quantum dots, polymers, graphene, carbon nanotubes, and biomolecules have been integrated into MOFs by appropriate strategies to form the composite/hybrid materials, during which process MOFs serve as a platform.[8] In comparison with the parent MOF counterparts, the above composite/hybrid materials display superior behaviors (catalytic, sensors, conductive, and medicine)[9] because of the synergistic properties. Particularly, functional organic molecular materials, such as organic dyes,[10] biomolecules,[11] organometallic compounds,[12] etc., are regarded as powerful tools in many practical applications including catalysis,[13] fluorescent probes,[14] and drug delivery.[15] Based on the confinement effect of MOFs, we can controllably combine MOF and molecules, which is an efficient way to prevent the molecules from serious leaching and aggregation. Hence, it is significant to develop methods to fabricate the molecules/MOFs composites which have a great variety of potential applications.
To date, three major synthetic strategies have been explored to embed functional organic molecules into MOFs. One is impregnation procedure in which the composite materials can be obtained through the loading of functional molecules into MOFs via capillary force. Ma’s group reported the successfully immobilization of microperoxidase-11 (MP-11) in mesoporous MOFs, which exhibited enhanced enzymatic catalysis performances due to the homogeneous distribution of MP-11.[16] But the challenge of this method is that usually the size of the functional molecules should be smaller than the MOFs pore dimension. In addition, postsynthetic strategy is introduced to integrate the functional molecules into MOFs matrix by different ways, such as electrostatic interactions[17] and coordination reaction.[18] For example, Lin’s group reported the design of the nanoscale MOF-based pH sensor for real-time pH measurements in live cells by covalent conjugating fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC) to a UiO nanoscale MOF.[18] The resulting FITC-conjugated nanoscale MOFs exhibited high FITC loadings, sensitive pH response, and efficient cellular uptake. However, the complicated modification process and the instability of MOF crystal structure restrict this postsynthetic method to be generally adopted in the formation of molecules/MOFs. Another strategy is the in situ encapsulation by the introduction of functional organic molecules in the growth of MOFs to construct the molecules/MOFs composites.[19] In this case, Zou and co-workers have combined MOF synthesis and molecule encapsulation in a one-pot process.[20] As a result, large drug molecules and dye molecules can be encapsulated in ZIF-8 crystals with well distribution. The as-prepared doxorubicin $@$ ZIF-8 was effectively delivered via pH-responsive release in cancer therapy. Nevertheless, the encapsulation of some active molecules is usually hindered by this strategy in that the involved metal salt and organic ligands will make these active molecules lose activity. Thus, a general and suitable method to incorporate functional organic molecules into MOFs needs to be developed given the demand and its extensive applications.
Herein, we report a facile encapsulation strategy that allows various functional organic molecules to be encapsulated in ZIF8-L (leaf-like zeolitic imidazolate framework) by in situ crystalline structure transformation of ZIF-L[21] under double solvent condition. ZIF-L is a 2D crystal lattice stacked layer-by-layer and is made up of the same building blocks as ZIF-8.[22] It is worth noting that ZIF-8 maintains a leaf-like morphology of ZIF-L, indicating that there is no dissolution and re-growth procedure, and the crystal structure transformation from ZIF-L to ZIF-8-L occurs in situ. In the transformation process of a 2D layered structure to a 3D framework, the functional organic molecules can be trapped by conversion of the pore structure. In this case, the fluorescent molecules were taken as an example to study the encapsulation process on account of their easy characterization. As a result, the fluorescent molecule/ZIF-8-L composites exhibit size selectivity in fluorescence quenchers because of the molecular sieving effect offered by the protection of microporous MOF. Compared with the methods of building functional organic molecules/MOFs mentioned above, this unique strategy shows a high efficiency on fluorescence sensors. This in situ crystalline structure transformation process exhibits a facile encapsulation of functional organic molecules by using organic solvents with no metal salt and organic ligands introduced, which will bring potential applications in the field of biochemistry and life science.
The controlled encapsulation of functional organic molecules in MOFs can be obtained through in situ crystalline structure transformation (Scheme 1). In this study, we chose the transformation from the nonporous ZIF-L to the porous ZIF-8-L process in that the tunable structure, and the fluorescent molecules, 1,4-diethoxybenzene (DB) were taken as an example to study the entire encapsulation procedure due to easy analysis. In a typical experiment, $4~\mathrm{mL}$ water solution of zinc nitrate $(59\mathrm{mg} $ $50\times10^{-3}\mathrm{{M})}$ ), $4\mathrm{mL}$ water solution of 2-methylimidazole ( $\mathrm{130mg}$ , $400\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M})$ ), and $0.1\mathrm{\mL}$ water solution of polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP, $\mathrm{1~mg)}$ were mixed briefly and allowed to react at ice-bath for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ under vigorous stirring. The as-prepared ZIF-L $(25~\mathrm{mg})$ was added into ethanol solution in the presence of DB $(2.5\mathrm{mg}$ , $6\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M~}$ ) and then kept at room temperature for $24\mathrm{h}$ under stirring conditions. The product was collected by centrifugation and re-dispersed in $32~\mathrm{mL}$ mixture of $N,N$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) and ethanol $(V_{\mathrm{DMF}};V_{\mathrm{ethanol}}=3{:}1)$ to afford a homogeneous mixture, and the reaction was kept at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ without stirring. Products were collected by centrifugation and then washed with ethanol. The molecules/MOF obtained through the above method was denoted as DB/ZIF8-L. ZIF-L with a leaf-like morphology showed the thickness of $160~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S1a, Supporting Information) and kept a welldefined crystal structure with the peak of (020), (023), (314), (243), and (443), respectively, which were in good consistent with the simulation of ZIF-L (Figure S1b, Supporting Information). More importantly, ZIF-8-L with a leaf-like morphology can be obtained by the in situ crystalline structure transformation of the ZIF-L, which was confirmed by the X-ray diffraction (XRD) data (Figure 2a).
To study the detailed process of crystal structure transformation, we chose different conditions and solvent for the conversion of ZIF-L to ZIF-8-L. The morphology, crystal structure, and porosity of the in situ crystalline structure transformation from ZIF-L to ZIF-8-L were further investigated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM), XRD analysis, and nitrogen-adsorption measurements, respectively. Under the alcohol solvent treatment, such as methanol and ethanol, ZIF-L nanosheets can rapidly transform to ZIF-8, resulting in the breakup of the leaflike morphology (Figure S2a,b, Supporting Information). However, using the DMF as solvent, the reaction rate of the transformation from ZIF-L to ZIF-8 was slow (Figure S2c, Supporting Information), and the mixture of ZIF-L and ZIF-8 was obtained after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure S3, Supporting Information). As a result of the solvent’s synergistic effect, ZIF-L nanosheets with a leaf-like morphology were gradually transformed to ZIF-8-L crystal from 0 to $24\mathrm{h}$ under double solvent condition (Figure 1a–f). ZIF-8-L crystal retained the leaf-like morphology and only the surface of ZIF-8-L nanosheets became more rough compared with that of ZIF-L, which may be attributed to the conversion of crystalline structure under the solvothermal treatment. In addition, the in situ crystalline structure transformation from ZIF-L to ZIF-8-L can also be confirmed by the XRD analysis (Figure 2a). Notably, the peak intensity of the ZIF-8-L appeared at $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , while that of ZIF-L decreased at the same time. The obtained product between 4 and $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ was the mixed phase of ZIF-L and ZIF8-L. After $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ , the characteristic peak of ZIF-L disappeared and after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ the pure ZIF-8-L was obtained. Moreover, the crystalline structure was directly transformed from ZIF-L to ZIF8-L since no new peaks from any other phases were observed in this process. Similarly, the peaks (011, 002, 112, 022, 013, and 114) of ZIF-8-L with leaf-like morphology were also in good agreement with those of intrinsic ZIF-8, which confirmed the phase purity during the in situ crystalline structure transformation. To further confirm the purity phase of ZIF-8-L, ZIF-8-L was then treated with grinding for 5 min and no obvious loss of the grinded ZIF-8-L crystallinity can be detected in the XRD pattern (Figure S4, Supporting Information). These characterizations exhibited that the ZIF-L was transformed to ZIF-8-L completely without other phase observed in this process. More interestingly, the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherm of ZIF-L to ZIF-8-L process demonstrated the recovery of porosity (Figure 2b) with the increase of the reaction time. At the initial stage, the gravimetric Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of ZIF-L is $63.711\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . As time increases, ZIFL-4h $(250.918\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ), ZIF-L-8h $(628.758\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , ZIF-L-12h $(788.360~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , and ZIF-L-18h ( $1145.331\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ , showed an obvious increase in BET surface areas. The as-prepared ZIF-8-L with the leaf-like morphology displayed a BET surface area of $1399.5~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , yielding the result similar to that of the directly synthesized ZIF-8. The increase of the special surface area may be attributed to the crystalline structure transformation from ZIF-L to ZIF-8, during which process more accessible pores and internal surface area were produced.[23] At the same time, the conversion of pore-size distribution during transformation from ZIF-L to ZIF-8-L was obvious, with the ZIF-L pore volume of $0.0005\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ Å and ZIF-8-L up to $0.316\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ (Figure S5, Supporting Information). These results indicated the recovery of the in situ crystalline structure under double solvent condition. More interestingly, we find that the double solvent used in in situ crystalline transformation strategy can be successfully extended to ZIF-L-Co. The ZIF-L-Co can transform to ZIF-67 with the leaf-like morphology (ZIF-67-L) (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Furthermore, serving as a host matrix, ZIF-8-L with a well-defined leaf-like morphology can exhibit size selectivity in fluorescent quenchers because of the ZIF-8-L molecular sieving effect on fluorescent sensors.
Fluorescent molecules, DB, were effectively encapsulated in ZIF-8-L during the in situ crystalline structure transformation process. The crystal structure, morphology, and fluorescence of DB/ZIF-8-L composites were further characterized by XRD, SEM analysis, and fluorescence measurements, respectively. The leaf-like morphology of DB/ZIF-8-L can be retained (Figure 3a). Simultaneously, as revealed by XRD measurements, DB/ZIF-8-L composites exhibited a well-defined crystal structure which was identical to the simulation patterns of ZIF-8 (Figure 3b). In addition, the peak of the DB/ZIF-8-L composite $(332~\mathrm{nm})$ (Figure 4c) was shifted slightly from that of free DB molecules $\phantom{+}326~\mathrm{nm})$ (Figure 4a), which was consistent with the fact that the photoluminescence energies of these fluorescent molecules are sensitive to their immediate environment offered by ZIF-8-L with the emission wavelength of $342~\mathrm{nm}$ . Compared with the as-prepared ZIF-8-L, the DB/ZIF8-L showed good fluorescence properties for the incorporated DB molecules in ZIF-8-L matrix. Notably, the general encapsulation strategy can also be successfully extended to other fluorescent molecules, endowing the molecules/MOF composites with new potential applications. For example, benzophenone (BP) as a fluorescent molecule can also be effectively integrated into ZIF-8-L through the in situ crystalline structure transformation strategy from ZIF-L to ZIF-8-L (Figure S7, Supporting Information). Hence, different fluorescent molecules can be controllably encapsulated in ZIF8-L, indicating the versatility and potential applications of this strategy in the field of chemical sensing, drug delivery, and catalysis.
The DB/ZIF-8-L composites as the fluorescent sensor exhibited the specific size selectivity offered by ZIF-8-L and the fluorescent sensing from fluorescent molecules. To evaluate the effects of encapsulation, halogens with different molecular sizes were used as fluorescent quenchers to quench the fluorescence of DB by molecular interaction (Figure 4b). As shown in Figure 4d, the fluorescence measurements showed that iodomethane molecules can rapidly access the pore of ZIF-8-L to quench the emission of DB, indicating that there was no obvious diffusion limitation caused by the ZIF-8-L matrix. In contrast, as a result of the molecular sieving effects of ZIF-8-L, the fluorescence of DB/ZIF-8-L cannot be quenched under the treatment of the large and long-chain molecules of 5-(bromomethyl)undecane (Figure 4d). Notably, the composites maintained the well-defined crystal structure and morphology after interaction with quenchers (Figure S8, Supporting Information). Different from the above methods, we also integrated fluorescent molecules into ZIF-8 via impregnation method and in situ growth method. For impregnation method, after large molecules of 5-(bromomethyl)undecane treatment, only $65\%$ emission of DB/ZIF-8(S) composites can be maintained, while the fluorescence emission of DB/ZIF-8(I) remained $70\%$ under the same condition (Figure S9, Supporting Information). These results indicated that our procedure exhibited good encapsulation efficiency in comparison with the traditional methods of preparing the molecules/MOFs composites.
In conclusion, we have demonstrated a facile and general strategy for the encapsulation of functional organic molecules in MOFs matrices by in situ crystalline structure transformation under double solvent conditions. The as-prepared molecule/ ZIF-8-L composites with a leaf-like morphology exhibit good fluorescence properties due to the generous encapsulation procedure of fluorescent molecules. In addition, as a fluorescence sensor, the molecules/ZIF-8-L composites show a good selectivity in fluorescent quenchers in that the fluorescent molecules are completely encapsulated in ZIF-8-L matrices. Compared with the impregnation method and the in situ encapsulation method, this strategy exhibits a high efficiency and selectivity in the fluorescence sensor. Furthermore, such an encapsulation strategy can also be extended to various kinds of molecules and MOFs, thus providing a new fascinating perspective for the development of functionalized MOF composite materials with new potential applications.
